    {
      "id": "7a49051164dc2277b9f1d8226021c5495e74dd59",
      "tag": "autogenerated:ai-review",
      "author": {
        "_account_id": 1345,
        "name": "Gerrit AI review for Lustre",
        "display_name": "AI review",
        "email": "gaireview@linuxhacker.ru",
        "username": "aireview",
        "status": "Contact Oleg Drokin (green@whamcloud.com) with any questions on this account"
      },
      "date": "2026-11-06 05:50:43.000000000",
      "message": "Patch Set 1:\n\n(4 comments)\n\nAI code review results below, please doublecheck\n\nThe code changes are safe: every new error path drops the lock, the buffers and (for writes) the grants correctly. Some concerns, see inline. The only new check that can actually trigger is the zero-buffer case, and the commit message doesn't describe it. A refresh is worth it mainly for the commit message; the inline minors can go along with it.\n\nThis is a pre-existing issue, not caused by this patch, and could be a separate change: a niobuf with rnb_len == 0 is never rejected before dt_bufs_get(). On ldiskfs, osd_bufs_get() then reads lnb[npages - 1] with npages == 0. For the first niobuf that is tbc->local[-1], outside the allocation. Rejecting zero-length niobufs in tgt_io_data_unpack(), next to the existing ioo_bufcnt checks, would cover both OSDs and the DoM path in mdt_io.c. It would also give a sanity test (like 819a/819b) a simple way to exercise the new -EPROTO path.",
      "accounts_in_message": [],
      "_revision_number": 1
    },
